Mild Contact Dermatitis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Lesions confined to exposure site, Intense pruritus, Well-demarcated margins

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-025
Reason for Visit: Acute, intensely itchy, weeping rash on the abdomen.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting an acute, rapidly evolving, highly pruritic vesicular eruption localized to the abdomen, accompanied by weeping and moderate distress. While localized, the intense burning and pruritus are causing significant sleep disruption and anxiety regarding a systemic allergic reaction or shingles. Urgent home assessment is indicated to rapidly differentiate the rash from Herpes Zoster, definitively rule out evolving systemic anaphylaxis, and aggressively institute topical therapies to manage the severe localized histaminergic response. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of secondary excoriation and infection, strictly fulfilling the criteria for MBS urgent after-hours item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: My stomach is covered in these weeping blisters that itch so intensely it feels like burning.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 48 hours ago following the application of a new metallic belt buckle directly against the skin. The patient initially noted a mild burning sensation, which linearly evolved over 24 hours into well-demarcated erythematous papules and vesicles strictly confined to the area of exposure. The lesions are intensely pruritic. In the last 12 hours, several vesicles have ruptured, resulting in weeping and oozing, followed by localized dryness and crusting. The rash has an explicitly geometric, rectangular margin matching the metallic irritant. There is an absolute absence of spread to unexposed areas. Denies fever, generalized hives, or shortness of breath.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High distress secondary to severe pruritus.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 124/80 mmHg | HR: 86 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Visibly distracted by the abdominal itch, frequently attempting to scratch through clothing.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Positive epicutaneous patch testing for specific allergens would assist in confirming the diagnosis chronologically, but is not acutely indicated.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination of the peri-umbilical abdomen reveals a 5cm x 4cm sharply demarcated, rectangular erythematous plaque. The surface is studded with multiple pinpoint vesicles, some of which are ruptured and actively weeping clear serous fluid. The margins are distinctly geometric and align perfectly with the patient's reported belt buckle exposure. No dermatomal distribution is present. The surrounding skin is entirely clear. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally; no wheeze. Oropharynx is clear.
Environmental Observations: The offending metallic belt is visible on a nearby chair.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has applied a basic calamine lotion which provided only transient, superficial relief.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Mild Contact Dermatitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Herpes Zoster (Shingles) - Definitively ruled out as the eruption crosses the midline, forms a distinct geometric shape corresponding to an external object rather than following a dermatomal distribution, and lacks severe neuropathic prodromal pain.
Impetigo - Ruled out by the intense predominance of pruritus over pain, the clear serous weeping rather than honey-colored purulent crusting, and the sharp, unnatural geometric borders.
Atopic Dermatitis - Unlikely given the sudden acute onset explicitly linked to a singular, novel exposure in a non-flexural area (abdomen), without a widespread history of chronic xerosis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The explosive onset of a highly pruritic, vesicular abdominal rash generated significant patient distress and necessitated an urgent home evaluation to exclude Herpes Zoster or early systemic hypersensitivity. Direct clinical examination confirmed a classic delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction (allergic contact dermatitis) directly mapped to a specific exogenous exposure. By definitively diagnosing the benign, localized nature of the rash and immediately prescribing potent topical corticosteroids to arrest the severe local inflammatory response, an unnecessary emergency department presentation was successfully averted. This essential diagnosis and targeted pain management could not safely wait until standard consulting hours, fulfilling the criteria for MBS items 585-600.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Betamethasone valerate 0.1% ointment to be applied strictly to the red, weeping areas twice daily for a maximum of 7 days. Advised Loratadine 10mg daily to assist with the histaminergic pruritus.
Positive Behavioral Change: To permanently eliminate this trigger, coat the back of all metallic belt buckles and jean buttons with two layers of clear nail polish. This acts as an invisible, physical barrier; physically separating the nickel alloy from the sweat on your skin completely halts the chemical reaction that causes the blistering rash.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 7 days if the weeping does not cease or if surrounding redness begins to spread.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department or Urgent Care immediately if the redness rapidly spreads beyond the original rectangular shape, becomes hot and deeply painful, or if you develop a fever and chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; formal patch testing by a dermatologist is the gold standard for definitive allergen identification if recurrent.
Clinical Course: The active weeping will cease within 24 to 48 hours of utilizing the potent steroid ointment. The redness and itch will fade over 7 to 10 days, followed by significant localized skin peeling as the dead epidermis sheds.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if you notice thick, yellow pus draining from the blisters, indicating a secondary bacterial infection from scratching.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: Your immune system recognized the metal (likely nickel) as a toxic invader and launched a massive attack, causing the skin to blister and weep. The steroid ointment suppresses this localized immune overreaction directly at the site.
Actionable Steps: Strictly avoid scrubbing the area with soap; gently rinse with lukewarm water only. Do not pop intact blisters, as the blister roof is nature's sterile biological dressing protecting the raw skin underneath.

